{
  "dom_id": "step-q341637",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Which of the following functions are piecewise continuous on $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x,\\,g(x)=\u230a\\frac{x}{3}\u230b,\\,h(x)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & \u2308x\u2309\\text{\u00a0is even} \\\\ -1,\\, & \u2308x\u2309\\text{\u00a0is odd}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $u(x)$ is the unit step function, and $\u2308x\u2309$ is the ceiling function.",
  "worked_solution": "Suppose $I\u2286R.$ A function $f:I\\to R$ is piecewise continuous on $I$ if it is piecewise continuous on every closed and bounded subinterval $[a,b]\u2282I.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ Consider the closed subinterval $x\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ Notice that the one-sided limits at $x=\\pi $ are infinite: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{-}}f(x)=+\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {\\pi }^{+}}f(x)=-\\infty .\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=\u2026,-6,-3,0,3,6,\u2026,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]\u2282[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042449/assets/370ce6bc297340f28b1e5bcd4a7e0ca3ce5de6edac660e143ac70ba615184765.png)\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ The function has jump discontinuities at $x=0,1,2,\u2026,$ and any closed interval $[a,b]\u2282[0,\\infty )$ will contain finitely many discontinuities.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042449/assets/9b7758701595db784d2d9aeb59cc823316501748976ccbed41980fa29ad0a892.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ and $h$ only.\"",
